.TH DEPENGINE 1 "2026-07-10" "depengine" "Tools"
.SH NAME
depengine \- distro-agnostic dependency installation engine guided by schema.toml
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B depengine
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
depengine is a single-binary dependency installer written in Go.
You describe \fIwhat\fR to install in a declarative
.BR schema.toml ;
the engine decides \fIhow\fR to install each tool by trying methods in
order (native, cargo, go, pip, git, http, ...) until one succeeds,
respecting per-distro conditions, inter-tool dependencies, and
post-installation hooks. The binary is static with no runtime
dependencies on the target machine.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install " [flags]"
Installs all tools from the schema, respecting
.BR method_order ,
.BR when ,
.BR requires ,
and topological ordering. See OPTIONS below for flags.
.TP
.B validate " [flags]"
Validates the schema structure and optionally the system environment.
Reports unknown methods, unknown placeholders, and missing tools.
.TP
.B init " [flags]"
Initializes a new depengine project by creating a minimal
.B depengine.toml
or
.B schema.toml
scaffold in the current directory. Use
.B \-\-schema
to specify a custom path.
.TP
.B check " <tool>"
Checks whether a specific tool is installed on the machine. Exits 0
if installed, 1 if not found.
.TP
.B status " [flags]"
Shows the installation status of tools by comparing the state file
against the schema. Reports installed, missing, orphaned, and
outdated tools.
.TP
.B remove " <tool> | --all"
Removes a tool using the adapter that originally installed it. Use
.B --all
to remove every tracked tool.
.TP
.B forget " <tool>"
Removes a tool from the state file without touching the system. Useful
when a tool was removed manually or the state is out of sync.
.TP
.B version
Prints the version and a short description.
.TP
.B completion " bash|zsh|fish"
Prints the shell completion script for the given shell. Source the
output in your shell rc file (e.g.
.BR "source <(depengine completion bash)" )
to enable tab completion.
.TP
.B undo " [flags]"
Reverts the last
.B install
by restoring the previous state snapshot and removing tools that were
added since that snapshot.
.TP
.B sbom " [flags]"
Exports the current depengine state as a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)
in CycloneDX 1.5 or SPDX 2.3 JSON format. Each installed tool becomes a
component/package in the BOM. Use
.B \-\-format spdx
to switch to SPDX output. Useful for integration with vulnerability scanners
such as grype or trivy.
.TP
.B update " [flags]"
Resolves {latest} placeholders (git refs, http URLs) via the GitHub API and writes the pinned versions to \fBdepengine.lock\fR. Use \fB\-\-frozen\-lockfile\fR to abort if the lockfile does not exist.
.TP
.B why " <tool>"
Explains how a tool would be installed: for each method, reports the
status (\fBalready_installed\fR, \fBwould_install\fR,
\fBskip_unavailable\fR, \fBskip_when\fR). Supports \fB--json\fR for
programmatic consumption.
.TP
.B diff " [flags] [file1] [file2]"
Compares depengine state files between machines or arbitrary paths.
If no files are given, compares the current state with the file specified by
.BR \-\-other .
If one file is given, compares the current state with that file.
If two files are given, compares the two files directly.
.TP
.B graph " [flags]"
Outputs the dependency graph in one of three formats: Mermaid (flowchart),
DOT (Graphviz), or text (level groups). Use
.B \-\-only
to focus on a single tool and its transitive dependencies.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "install flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-schema " <path>"
Path to the schema file. Default: \fBschema.toml\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be installed without executing anything.
.TP
.BR \-\-verbose " | \fB\-v\fR"
Detailed per-tool output.
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Emit JSON output for programmatic consumption.
.TP
.BR \-\-only " <tool>"
Install only the specified tool.
.TP
.BR \-\-skip " <tools>"
Skip specific tools (comma-separated list).
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " <tag>"
Only install tools that have the given tag or no tags (e.g. \fB--profile=desktop\fR).
Useful for targeting different machine profiles from a single schema file.
.TP
.BR \-\-sort\-by " <field>"
Sort output by: \fBname\fR, \fBstatus\fR, or \fBmethod\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-level " <level>"
Log level: \fBdebug\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBwarn\fR, or \fBerror\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-diagnose
Diagnostic mode: implies \fB--dry-run\fR, \fB--verbose\fR, and
\fBdebug\fR log level.
.TP
.BR \-\-jobs " <n>"
Maximum concurrent installations within a topological level. Default: 1
(sequential).
.TP
.BR \-\-allow-arbitrary-code
Suppress security warnings for build scripts (arbitrary code execution).
.SS "validate flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-schema " <path>"
Path to the schema file. Default: \fBschema.toml\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-check\-env
Check whether required tools are available in the environment (PATH).
.TP
.BR \-\-format " <fmt>"
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default) or \fBjson\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-strict
Treat warnings as errors (exit code 1).
.SS "status flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-schema " <path>"
Override the schema path (default: from state file).
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Emit JSON output.
.TP
.BR \-\-orphans
Show only orphaned tools (installed but no longer in schema).
.SS "remove flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-all
Remove all tracked tools.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be removed without executing.
.SS "graph flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-schema " <path>"
Path to the schema file. Default: \fBschema.toml\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " <fmt>"
Output format: \fBmermaid\fR, \fBdot\fR, or \fBtext\fR (default).
.TP
.BR \-\-only " <tool>"
Show only the subgraph for the specified tool and its transitive
dependencies.
.TP
.BR \-\-skip " <tools>"
Skip specific tools (comma-separated list).
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " <tag>"
Show only tools with the given tag (e.g. \fB--profile=server\fR).
.SS "undo flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-list
List available snapshots with timestamps and tool counts.
.TP
.BR \-\-snapshot " <path>"
Restore a specific snapshot file instead of the most recent one.
.SS "sbom flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-format " <fmt>"
Output format: \fBcyclonedx\fR (default) or \fBspdx\fR.
.SS "diff flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Output differences as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-\-other " <path>"
Path to the other state file to compare (used when no arguments are provided).
.SS "why flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Emit JSON output for programmatic consumption.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " <fmt>"
Output format: \fBtext\fR (default) or \fBjson\fR.
.SS "update flags"
.TP
.BR \-\-frozen\-lockfile
Abort if no lockfile exists (prevents resolving {latest} from network).
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be updated without executing.
.SS "schema-level per-tool method control"
.TP
.B method_prefer
List of methods to try first, before the defaults.
Example: \fBmethod_prefer = ["cargo"]\fR tries cargo before native.
.TP
.B method_only
Filters the tool's candidate methods: only the listed methods are tried,
in this order, and the native method is NOT attempted unless explicitly
listed — there is no native fallback. Global method_order is ignored for
this tool.
.TP
.B method_order
Deprecated. Synonym for \fBmethod_prefer\fR.

.SS "kind field (adapter dispatch override)"
.PP
The \fBkind\fR field overrides the adapter dispatch key inside a method block.
Normally the section key (e.g. \fBhttp-musl\fR) IS the kind. When you have two
methods that use the same adapter but differ only in their install source
(e.g. glibc vs musl builds), set \fBkind = "http"\fR so the second variant
resolves to the same adapter.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
[tools.restic]
  [tools.restic.http-musl]
  kind = "http"
  url = "https://example.com/musl"
  when = { libc = ["musl"] }
.fi
.PP
When \fBkind\fR is absent, the section key acts as the kind (backward
compatible). Non-string values are silently ignored.
.SS "when condition (platform gate by method)"
.PP
The \fBwhen\fR field inside a method declaration restricts which platforms
that method applies to. The condition is evaluated against facts detected
by \fBdetect_os.sh\fR at runtime. Semantics: AND across fields, OR within
each list. Empty fields are ignored. Absent \fBwhen\fR = always try.
.PP
Available fields:
.TP
.B distro_family
List of distro families (resolved clans). Example: \fB["arch"]\fR matches
Arch, EndeavourOS, Manjaro, CachyOS. Common values: arch, debian, fedora,
alpine, gentoo, void, suse, macos, freebsd, openbsd, netbsd, termux, android.
.TP
.B distro_id
List of exact distro IDs (case-insensitive). Example: \fB["ubuntu",
"debian"]\fR matches exactly those, not Mint or Pop!_OS.
.TP
.B arch
List of architectures (\fBuname -m\fR). Example: \fB["x86_64", "aarch64"]\fR.
.TP
.B os
List of operating systems (lowercase \fBuname -s\fR). Example:
\fB["linux"]\fR, \fB["darwin"]\fR, \fB["freebsd"]\fR.
.TP
.B kernel
List of kernel versions (\fBuname -r\fR). Exact match (case-insensitive).
Example: \fB["6.7.0-arch", "6.8.0"]\fR.
.TP
.B libc
List of C libraries. Uses \fBprefix\fR matching: \fB["glibc"]\fR matches
"glibc 2.35". Values: glibc, musl, uclibc, freebsd-libc.
.TP
.B init_system
List of init systems. Values: systemd, openrc, runit, sysvinit.
.TP
.B is_wsl
Three-state boolean: \fBtrue\fR (WSL only), \fBfalse\fR (non-WSL only),
omitted (ignored). Detected via /proc/version or WSL_DISTRO_NAME.
.TP
.B is_container
Three-state boolean: \fBtrue\fR (container only), \fBfalse\fR (non-container
only), omitted (ignored). Detected via /.dockerenv, cgroup, etc.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
# AUR only on Arch, HTTP elsewhere
[tools.DepartureMono]
  [tools.DepartureMono.aur]
  pkg  = "otf-departure-mono-nerd"
  when = { distro_family = ["arch"] }

  [tools.DepartureMono.http]
  url = "https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts/releases/download/{latest}/DepartureMono.zip"

# Different binary per libc (glibc vs musl/Alpine)
[tools.restic]
  [tools.restic.http]
  url = "https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/{latest}/restic_{latest}_linux_{arch}.bz2"
  when = { arch = ["x86_64","aarch64"], os = ["linux"], libc = ["glibc"] }

  [tools.restic.http-musl]
  kind = "http"
  url = "https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/{latest}/restic_{latest}_linux_{arch}_musl.bz2"
  when = { arch = ["x86_64","aarch64"], os = ["linux"], libc = ["musl"] }

# Skip native in containers
[tools.neovim]
  [tools.neovim.native]
  when = { is_container = false }

  [tools.neovim.http]
  url = "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases/download/stable/nvim-linux-{arch}.tar.gz"
  when = { is_container = true }
.EE
.PP
Tip: run \fBdepengine why <tool>\fR to see why a method was
skipped (\fBskip_when\fR) or would be attempted.

.SS "ecosystem buckets (python/node shorthand)"
.PP
Buckets expand to all methods in that ecosystem at once, avoiding repetition
across \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR and \fBuv\fR (python) or \fBnpm\fR, \fBpnpm\fR
and \fBbun\fR (node).
.TP
.B python
Expands to \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR and \fBuv\fR.
.TP
.B node
Expands to \fBnpm\fR, \fBpnpm\fR and \fBbun\fR.
.PP
Bucket values accept three shapes:
.br
\fBtrue\fR \— each method uses the tool name as \fBpkg\fR.
.br
\fB"string"\fR \— each method gets the string as \fBpkg\fR.
.br
\fB{ pkg = ..., when = ... }\fR \— each method gets a clone of the config map.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
ruff = { python = true }               # ≡ { pip = "ruff", pipx = "ruff", uv = "ruff" }
organize = { python = "organize-tool" } # ≡ pip/pipx/uv with pkg="organize-tool"
prettier = { node = true }             # ≡ { npm = "prettier", pnpm = "prettier", bun = "prettier" }
.EE
.PP
Explicit methods are NOT overridden by the bucket:
\fBorganize = { pip = "organize-tool", python = true }\fR keeps
\fBpip = "organize-tool"\fR and only expands \fBpipx\fR/\fBuv\fR.
Use \fBpython = false\fR to disable expansion.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Build and validate"
.EX
go build -o depengine .
./depengine validate
.EE
.SS "Validate with environment check and JSON output"
.EX
./depengine validate --check-env --format=json
.EE
.SS "Install all tools from a custom schema"
.EX
./depengine install --schema my-tools.toml
.EE
.SS "Install a single tool verbosely"
.EX
./depengine install --only nvim --verbose
.EE
.SS "Dry run with sorted output"
.EX
./depengine install --diagnose
.EE
.SS "Install with profile (desktop-only)"
.EX
./depengine install --profile=desktop
.EE
.SS "Check if a tool is installed"
.EX
./depengine check nvim
.EE
.SS "Show installation status"
.EX
./depengine status
./depengine status --json
./depengine status --orphans
.EE
.SS "Remove a tool"
.EX
./depengine remove bat
./depengine remove --all --dry-run
.EE
.SS "Enable shell completion"
.EX
# Bash
source <(depengine completion bash)

# Zsh
source <(depengine completion zsh)

# Fish
depengine completion fish | source
.EE
.SS "Show dependency graph"
.EX
./depengine graph
./depengine graph --format=mermaid
./depengine graph --format=dot --only=tool_name
./depengine graph --profile=desktop
.EE
.SS "Export SBOM"
.EX
./depengine sbom
./depengine sbom --format=spdx
.EE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEPENGINE_DETECT_SCRIPT
Path to the
.B detect_os.sh
script. Search order: an embedded copy of the script is used by default;
if this variable is set it overrides the embedded copy; otherwise the
engine looks for
.B scripts/detect_os.sh
alongside the binary, then
.B detect_os.sh
on \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B DEPENGINE_MANIFEST
Path to the personal manifest file, overriding the default XDG discovery
(\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/depengine/manifest.toml\fR, or
\fB~/.config/depengine/manifest.toml\fR if unset).
.TP
.B DEPENGINE_TRACE_ID
Trace ID propagated to subprocesses for correlated logging.
.TP
.B DEPENGINE_LOG_JSON
Set to \fB1\fR to enable JSON output for the structured logger.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for the state file. The state file is stored at
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/depengine/state.json\fR, falling back to
\fB~/.local/state/depengine/state.json\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B schema.toml
The declarative schema describing tools and installation methods.
Shipped with the project as a reference template.
.TP
.B scripts/detect_os.sh
System detection script invoked at runtime to gather OS, architecture,
distro, libc, and init-system facts.
.TP
.BI $XDG_STATE_HOME/depengine/state.json
State file tracking which tools were installed, by which method, and
when. Used by the
.B status
and
.B remove
commands.
.TP
.B scripts/depengine-completion.bash
.B scripts/depengine-completion.zsh
.B scripts/depengine-completion.fish
Shell completion scripts emitted by the
.B completion
command.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
0
Success — all tools installed or already present.
.TP
1
Some tool failed to install, or strict-mode validation found warnings.
.TP
2
Schema error — invalid TOML, validation failure, unknown method, or
unknown placeholder.
.TP
3
Runtime error —
.B detect_os.sh
not found, state I/O failure, or other infrastructure error.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
The project README and
.B docs/cheatsheet.md
contain the complete
.B schema.toml
reference, method examples, and placeholder documentation.
